BANK.push(
{"id": "a1", "d": 2, "q": "A company deploys its primary workload in East US and its DR copy in West US, which is East US's region pair. Which benefit do they get from using the region pair rather than an arbitrary second region?", "o": ["Planned platform updates are rolled out to only one region of the pair at a time", "Data written to East US is synchronously replicated to West US for every service", "Traffic between the two paired regions is free of any bandwidth or transfer charges", "Resources in the pair share a single availability zone layout to simplify failover"], "a": [0], "e": "Region pairs get sequential platform updates (one region at a time), prioritized recovery of one region in a broad outage, and data residency within the same geography (with rare exceptions). Replication between them depends on the service and is usually asynchronous; inter-region traffic is billed; zones are per region."},
{"id": "a2", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about Azure region pairs is TRUE?", "o": ["Every Azure region has exactly one paired region in the same geography", "Some newer regions have no pair and rely on availability zones instead", "Region pairs are always in different geographies to maximize separation", "You can choose any other region as your region's pair from the portal"], "a": [1], "e": "Microsoft defines region pairs; you can't choose them. Most are in the same geography for data residency. Several newer regions are not paired and are built with availability zones for in-region resiliency."},
{"id": "a3", "d": 2, "q": "In a broad outage that affects multiple regions, what does Azure do with region pairs?", "o": ["Restores all affected regions at the same time", "Prioritizes recovery of one region in each pair", "Automatically fails every customer workload to the paired region", "Moves all customer data to a third, unaffected geography"], "a": [1], "e": "During a broad outage, Azure prioritizes the recovery of one region out of every pair. Automatic failover of your workload isn't guaranteed; you plan and often trigger it yourself."},
{"id": "a4", "d": 2, "q": "A US federal agency needs Azure services in a physically isolated cloud instance operated by screened US personnel. Which option fits?", "o": ["Azure Government", "Azure Dedicated Host in East US", "Azure operated by 21Vianet", "A private endpoint to a US commercial region"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure Government is a sovereign cloud for US government agencies and partners, physically and logically isolated from commercial Azure and operated by screened US personnel. 21Vianet operates Azure in China."},
{"id": "a5", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about Azure in China is TRUE?", "o": ["It is operated by Microsoft from US datacenters", "It is operated by 21Vianet and is physically separated from global Azure", "It is the same instance as global Azure with Chinese language support", "It is part of Azure Government for allied nations"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure in China is operated by 21Vianet, physically separated from the rest of Azure. It is a sovereign region alongside Azure Government in the US."},
{"id": "a6", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about availability zones is TRUE?", "o": ["Each zone is a single rack inside one datacenter, with its own power feed", "Each zone is one or more datacenters with independent power, cooling, and networking", "Zones are spread across different Azure regions within the same geography for DR", "All Azure services automatically span every zone in a region with no configuration"], "a": [1], "e": "An availability zone is one or more datacenters with independent power, cooling, and networking, within a single region. Services may be zonal, zone-redundant, or non-regional; not all are automatically zone-redundant."},
{"id": "a7", "d": 2, "q": "What is the minimum number of availability zones in a region that supports them?", "o": ["Two", "Three", "Four", "Six"], "a": [1], "e": "Regions that support availability zones have a minimum of three separate zones."},
{"id": "a8", "d": 2, "q": "An architect pins a VM and its managed disk to Zone 1. Which type of zone-supported service deployment is this?", "o": ["Zone-redundant", "Zonal", "Non-regional", "Geo-redundant"], "a": [1], "e": "Zonal services are pinned to a specific zone (VMs, managed disks, IP addresses). Zone-redundant services replicate across zones automatically (for example, ZRS storage, zone-redundant SQL Database). Non-regional services are always available from Azure geographies."},
{"id": "a9", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO services are examples of zone-redundant offerings? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)", "Azure SQL Database with zone redundancy enabled", "A single VM deployed to Zone 2", "A managed disk attached to a zonal VM"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "ZRS and zone-redundant SQL Database automatically replicate across zones. A single VM and its disk are zonal."},
{"id": "a10", "d": 2, "q": "A team deploys three VMs across Zones 1, 2, and 3 of a region behind a zone-redundant load balancer. What does this protect against?", "o": ["The failure of an entire Azure region", "The failure of a single datacenter in the region", "An accidental delete of the resource group", "A misconfigured NSG that blocks all traffic"], "a": [1], "e": "Spreading across zones protects against a datacenter or zone failure. It doesn't protect against region loss (needs multi-region), accidental deletion (needs locks and backups), or misconfiguration."},
{"id": "a11", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about Azure datacenters is MOST accurate?", "o": ["Customers choose which datacenter building in a region their resources will run in", "Customers deploy to regions (and optionally zones), not to individual datacenters", "Each Azure region is a single datacenter, and zones are separate rooms inside it", "Customers can schedule visits to inspect the racks that host their own VMs on request"], "a": [1], "e": "Customers don't pick individual datacenters. Datacenters are grouped into availability zones and regions, and you deploy to those."},
{"id": "a12", "d": 2, "q": "A resource group was created in West Europe. Which statement about the resources in it is TRUE?", "o": ["All resources in it must also be deployed in the West Europe region", "Its resources can be in other regions; its location stores its metadata", "Resources in it can only be moved to another group by recreating them", "The group's location sets the billing currency for all of its resources"], "a": [1], "e": "Resources in a resource group can be in different regions. The resource group's location is where its metadata is stored. Many resources can be moved between groups without recreation."},
{"id": "a13", "d": 2, "q": "An admin deletes a resource group that contains a VM, a VNet, and a storage account. What happens?", "o": ["Only the resource group container is deleted; the resources become orphaned", "All resources in the group are deleted", "The resources move to the subscription's default resource group", "Deletion fails unless the resources are deleted first"], "a": [1], "e": "Deleting a resource group deletes every resource in it, which is why resource groups are useful for lifecycle management and why delete locks matter."},
{"id": "a14", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about resource groups is FALSE?", "o": ["A resource can belong to only one resource group at a time", "Resource groups can be nested inside other resource groups", "Resources can be moved from one resource group to another", "Role assignments on a resource group are inherited by its resources"], "a": [1], "e": "Resource groups can't be nested. A resource belongs to one group, many resources can be moved, and RBAC on a group flows down to its resources."},
{"id": "a15", "d": 2, "q": "A team's dev, test, and prod environments for one app all live in one resource group. They want to tear down dev without risk to prod and give different teams access to each. What is the BEST change?", "o": ["Put each environment in its own resource group", "Add tags to each resource marking its environment", "Use one resource group and apply a delete lock to prod resources", "Move prod to a different region inside the same resource group"], "a": [0], "e": "Resource groups are the natural unit for lifecycle and access. Separate groups let you delete dev in one action and assign RBAC per environment. Tags don't control access or deletion; a lock helps but doesn't solve access or lifecycle cleanly."},
{"id": "a16", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO boundaries does an Azure subscription provide? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Billing boundary", "Access control boundary", "Network isolation boundary", "Data residency boundary"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Subscriptions are a billing boundary and an access control boundary. Network isolation comes from VNets; residency comes from region choice."},
{"id": "a17", "d": 2, "q": "An organization wants separate invoices for its Marketing and Engineering departments, and different access policies for each. What is the simplest approach?", "o": ["Create separate subscriptions for each department", "Create separate resource groups in one subscription", "Create separate VNets in one subscription", "Create separate Microsoft Entra tenants for each department"], "a": [0], "e": "Separate subscriptions give separate billing and access boundaries. Resource groups don't separate invoices (though tags and cost analysis can split costs); separate tenants add a lot of complexity."},
{"id": "a18", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about the relationship between Microsoft Entra tenants and Azure subscriptions is TRUE?", "o": ["A subscription can trust several tenants at once for sign-in to its resources", "A tenant can have many subscriptions, but each subscription trusts only one tenant", "Each tenant can be associated with only one subscription at any point in time", "Subscriptions and tenants are the same object viewed from billing and identity"], "a": [1], "e": "A subscription trusts exactly one Entra tenant for identities. One tenant can be associated with many subscriptions."},
{"id": "a19", "d": 2, "q": "A company has 40 subscriptions and wants a policy restricting resources to EU regions on all of them, including future subscriptions for EU business units. What should they use?", "o": ["Assign the policy to each existing subscription individually, then repeat for new ones", "Group the subscriptions under a management group and assign the policy there once", "Assign the policy to each resource group in every subscription so nothing is missed", "Create the policy as a required tag on each subscription so it applies to resources"], "a": [1], "e": "Policies and RBAC assigned to a management group are inherited by all subscriptions under it, including subscriptions added later."},
{"id": "a20", "d": 2, "q": "What is the correct order of the Azure scope hierarchy, from broadest to narrowest?", "o": ["Subscription > Management group > Resource group > Resource", "Management group > Subscription > Resource group > Resource", "Management group > Resource group > Subscription > Resource", "Resource group > Subscription > Management group > Resource"], "a": [1], "e": "Management groups contain subscriptions, subscriptions contain resource groups, and resource groups contain resources."},
{"id": "a21", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about management groups is TRUE?", "o": ["A subscription can have more than one parent management group at a time", "The tree supports up to six levels of depth, not counting root or subscriptions", "Management groups can contain resource groups directly, without subscriptions", "A tenant is limited to 10 management groups unless a support case raises it"], "a": [1], "e": "Each management group and subscription has one parent. The tree supports up to six levels of depth (excluding the root and subscription levels), and a directory can hold 10,000 management groups. Management groups contain subscriptions or other management groups."},
{"id": "a22", "d": 2, "q": "What sits at the very top of the management group hierarchy in a tenant?", "o": ["The billing account", "The root management group", "The first subscription created", "The Global Administrator account"], "a": [1], "e": "Every directory has a single top-level root management group; all management groups and subscriptions roll up to it."},
{"id": "a23", "d": 2, "q": "An admin assigns the Reader role at a management group. A user later gets Contributor on one resource group in a subscription under that management group. What can the user do in that resource group?", "o": ["Only read, because the higher scope wins", "Read and write, because role assignments are additive", "Nothing, because conflicting roles cancel out", "Only write, because the narrower scope replaces inherited roles"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure RBAC is additive. The user has Reader everywhere under the management group and Contributor on that resource group, so effective permissions there include Contributor's write actions."},
{"id": "a24", "d": 2, "q": "A developer needs a long-running Windows app with custom COM components and a registry-heavy installer. Which compute option fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Functions", "Azure Container Instances with a Linux image", "Azure Virtual Machines", "Azure Logic Apps"], "a": [2], "e": "Apps that need deep OS customization (registry, COM, custom installers) fit VMs best. Functions and Logic Apps are serverless; a Linux container won't run Windows COM components."},
{"id": "a25", "d": 2, "q": "A team wants to run a single container for a short job with no orchestration, no VM management, and billing per second. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Kubernetes Service", "Azure Container Instances", "Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets", "Azure Virtual Desktop"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure Container Instances is the simplest way to run a container in Azure, with no VMs or orchestrator to manage, billed per second. AKS is for orchestrating many containers."},
{"id": "a26", "d": 2, "q": "A company runs dozens of microservices in containers and needs rolling updates, service discovery, and fine-grained control over the orchestrator. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Container Instances", "Azure Kubernetes Service", "Azure Functions", "Azure App Service on a Basic plan"], "a": [1], "e": "AKS provides managed Kubernetes for orchestrating many containers with full Kubernetes control. ACI runs individual containers; Functions is event-driven code."},
{"id": "a27", "d": 2, "q": "A team wants to run containerized microservices that scale to zero, with built-in autoscaling on HTTP traffic or events, but does NOT want to manage Kubernetes. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Container Apps", "Azure Kubernetes Service", "Azure Virtual Machines", "Azure Batch"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure Container Apps is a serverless container platform built for microservices, with event-driven autoscaling and scale to zero, without exposing Kubernetes management. AKS gives you Kubernetes to manage."},
{"id": "a28", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement correctly compares containers and VMs?", "o": ["Containers virtualize the hardware, while VMs virtualize the operating system", "Containers share the host OS kernel and start faster; each VM runs a full guest OS", "Containers always give stronger isolation between workloads than VMs on the same host", "VMs can't be scaled automatically in Azure, but containers can scale on any platform"], "a": [1], "e": "Containers virtualize the OS (share the kernel), so they're lighter and start faster. VMs virtualize hardware and each runs a full OS. VMs can be autoscaled with VMSS."},
{"id": "a29", "d": 2, "q": "A web tier must automatically add identical VMs when CPU exceeds 70% and remove them when load drops, behind a load balancer. Which feature fits BEST?", "o": ["Availability sets", "Virtual Machine Scale Sets", "Azure Dedicated Host", "Proximity placement groups"], "a": [1], "e": "VM Scale Sets create and manage a group of identical, load-balanced VMs that scale automatically. Availability sets spread VMs across fault and update domains but don't autoscale."},
{"id": "a30", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about availability sets is TRUE?", "o": ["They spread VMs across multiple Azure regions to survive a regional outage", "They spread VMs across fault domains and update domains within a datacenter", "They automatically add or remove VMs in the set based on CPU or memory load", "They add an hourly charge for the set itself on top of the cost of each VM"], "a": [1], "e": "Availability sets spread VMs across fault domains (separate power and network) and update domains (separate maintenance groups). There's no cost for the availability set itself; you pay for the VMs."},
{"id": "a31", "d": 2, "q": "What does a fault domain in an availability set represent?", "o": ["A group of VMs that are rebooted together during planned maintenance", "A group of VMs that share a common power source and network switch", "A separate Azure region for disaster recovery", "A logical group of VMs with the same OS image"], "a": [1], "e": "A fault domain groups VMs that share power and network hardware, much like a rack. Update domains define which VMs are rebooted together during planned maintenance."},
{"id": "a32", "d": 2, "q": "A planned maintenance event requires host reboots. How does an availability set reduce impact?", "o": ["It migrates VMs to another region during maintenance", "It reboots only one update domain at a time", "It defers all maintenance until the customer approves it", "It snapshots VMs and restores them after the reboot"], "a": [1], "e": "During planned maintenance, only one update domain is rebooted at a time, so some VMs in the set stay up. Region migration and indefinite deferral aren't part of availability sets."},
{"id": "a33", "d": 2, "q": "A VM uses Premium SSD for its OS and data disks. Which single-instance VM SLA applies, if no availability set or zones are used?", "o": ["99.5%", "99.9%", "99.95%", "99.99%"], "a": [1], "e": "A single-instance VM with Premium SSD (or Premium SSD v2 / Ultra Disk) for all disks has a 99.9% SLA. Two or more VMs in an availability set get 99.95%, and two or more across availability zones get 99.99%."},
{"id": "a34", "d": 2, "q": "An architect needs the highest VM connectivity SLA Azure offers for a two-VM web tier in one region. Which deployment should they use?", "o": ["Two VMs placed in the same availability set", "Two VMs across two or more availability zones", "One VM using Premium SSD for all of its disks", "Two VMs placed on the same Azure Dedicated Host"], "a": [1], "e": "Two or more VMs across availability zones in the same region carry a 99.99% SLA, higher than an availability set (99.95%) or a single VM (up to 99.9%)."},
{"id": "a35", "d": 2, "q": "A company wants to give 300 call center agents a Windows desktop they can reach from any device, with multiple users sharing each VM to save cost. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Virtual Desktop with Windows Enterprise multi-session", "Azure VMs with a separate Windows Server VM for each agent", "Azure Bastion with RDP sessions shared across all the agents", "Azure App Service for Windows hosting a remote desktop gateway"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure Virtual Desktop supports Windows 11 and Windows 10 Enterprise multi-session, letting many users share a VM. Bastion is secure RDP/SSH access for admins, not a desktop platform."},
{"id": "a36", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO statements about Azure Virtual Desktop are TRUE? (Choose two.)", "o": ["It supports Windows Enterprise multi-session, so many users can share one VM", "User profiles can be kept separate from the session host VMs", "It requires every user to have a dedicated physical server", "It can only be accessed from Windows devices"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "AVD offers Windows 11 and Windows 10 Enterprise multi-session, where several users share one session host, and separates user profiles (for example, with FSLogix) from session hosts. Users can connect from Windows, macOS, iOS, Android, and web browsers."},
{"id": "a37", "d": 2, "q": "Which resource is REQUIRED when you create an Azure VM?", "o": ["A public IP address so the VM can be managed", "A network interface attached to a VNet subnet", "A network security group applied to the NIC", "An Azure Load Balancer in front of the VM"], "a": [1], "e": "A VM needs a NIC attached to a VNet subnet, plus disks. A public IP, NSG, and load balancer are optional."},
{"id": "a38", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO resources are created or attached as part of a typical Azure VM deployment? (Choose two.)", "o": ["An OS disk", "A network interface", "An Azure Front Door profile", "An Application Gateway"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "A VM needs at least an OS disk and a NIC (plus a VNet/subnet). Front Door and Application Gateway are separate, optional load-balancing services."},
{"id": "a39", "d": 2, "q": "A team deletes a VM through the portal and later notices costs continuing. Which leftover resource is the MOST likely cause?", "o": ["The VM's compute reservation still being charged", "Managed disks and a static public IP left behind", "The VM's update domain still being allocated", "The availability zone fee for the deleted VM"], "a": [1], "e": "Deleting a VM doesn't necessarily delete its disks, NIC, or public IP unless you choose to. Orphaned managed disks and static public IPs keep billing."},
{"id": "a40", "d": 2, "q": "A team wants to host a Node.js web app with deployment slots, autoscale, and built-in CI/CD, without managing servers. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure App Service", "Azure Virtual Machines", "Azure Container Instances", "Azure Batch"], "a": [0], "e": "App Service is a PaaS web hosting platform with deployment slots, autoscale, and CI/CD integration. VMs need server management; ACI lacks slots and autoscale; Batch is for parallel jobs."},
{"id": "a41", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO types of applications can App Service host? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Web apps", "API apps", "Kubernetes control planes", "Domain controllers"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "App Service hosts web apps, API apps, WebJobs, and mobile back ends. It doesn't host Kubernetes control planes or AD domain controllers."},
{"id": "a42", "d": 2, "q": "A team already packages its web app as a Docker image and wants PaaS hosting with custom domains and TLS, but no orchestrator. Which option fits with LEAST effort?", "o": ["Run the container on App Service (Web App for Containers)", "Install Docker on a VM and manage the host and runtime yourself", "Deploy a full AKS cluster with an ingress controller for TLS", "Rewrite the app as Azure Functions so it runs without servers"], "a": [0], "e": "App Service can run custom containers with built-in custom domains, TLS, scaling, and slots. A VM adds OS management, AKS adds orchestration overhead, and rewriting for Functions is unnecessary work."},
{"id": "a43", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about Azure virtual networks is TRUE?", "o": ["A VNet spans every region in its geography by default for resiliency", "A VNet lives in one region but can be peered with VNets in other regions", "A VNet must contain exactly one subnet, and each subnet needs its own VNet", "VNets can't connect to on-premises networks without first peering to a hub"], "a": [1], "e": "A VNet lives in one region. Global VNet peering connects VNets across regions. VNets can have many subnets and can connect to on-premises through VPN Gateway or ExpressRoute."},
{"id": "a44", "d": 2, "q": "VNet A is peered with VNet B, and VNet B is peered with VNet C. Without any other configuration, can resources in VNet A reach VNet C through B?", "o": ["Yes, because peering relationships are transitive by default in Azure", "No, peering is non-transitive; A and C need direct peering or a routing hub", "Yes, as long as all three VNets are in the same subscription and region", "Only if VNet B has a public IP address and IP forwarding is turned on"], "a": [1], "e": "VNet peering is non-transitive. A to C requires direct peering or a hub with a network virtual appliance, Azure Firewall, or Virtual WAN routing traffic."},
{"id": "a45", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about VNet peering traffic is TRUE?", "o": ["It travels over the public internet but is protected with IPsec encryption", "It stays on the Microsoft backbone network and doesn't need any gateway", "It requires a VPN gateway deployed in each VNet that is part of the peering", "It only works between VNets in the same region; other regions need VPN"], "a": [1], "e": "Peered VNet traffic uses Microsoft's private backbone and doesn't need gateways. Global peering supports VNets in different regions."},
{"id": "a46", "d": 2, "q": "Two VNets have overlapping address spaces (both 10.0.0.0/16). What happens when you try to peer them?", "o": ["Peering succeeds and Azure NATs the overlap automatically", "Peering fails because address spaces can't overlap", "Peering succeeds but only the first VNet can initiate traffic", "Peering succeeds only with global peering"], "a": [1], "e": "Peered VNets must have non-overlapping address spaces. Plan IP addressing before connecting networks."},
{"id": "a47", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about Azure DNS is TRUE?", "o": ["You can register and buy new domain names directly in Azure DNS", "It hosts DNS zones that you manage with Azure tools, RBAC, and billing", "It only supports private zones for VNets, not public internet domains", "It replaces your registrar, so domains no longer need to be renewed"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure DNS hosts DNS zones (public and private) using Azure's global name server network and Azure tooling. It isn't a registrar; you buy domains elsewhere (or via App Service domains) and delegate them to Azure DNS."},
{"id": "a48", "d": 2, "q": "A company wants VMs in several VNets to resolve each other by custom names like db01.corp.internal without running its own DNS servers. Which feature fits BEST?", "o": ["An Azure DNS public zone for the internal names", "An Azure DNS private zone linked to the VNets", "Azure Traffic Manager with priority routing", "Azure Front Door with private origin routing"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure Private DNS zones provide name resolution inside linked VNets without custom DNS servers. Public zones are for internet-facing names; Traffic Manager and Front Door route traffic."},
{"id": "a49", "d": 2, "q": "A branch office needs an encrypted connection to an Azure VNet over the internet using its existing firewall. Which option fits BEST?", "o": ["Site-to-site VPN through Azure VPN Gateway", "Point-to-site VPN installed on each user's PC", "ExpressRoute private peering through a provider", "VNet peering between the branch and the VNet"], "a": [0], "e": "Site-to-site VPN connects an on-premises device to an Azure VPN gateway over IPsec across the internet. Point-to-site is per-device; ExpressRoute is a private circuit through a provider; peering connects VNets."},
{"id": "a50", "d": 2, "q": "A remote developer on a laptop needs to reach VMs on a private subnet in Azure from home, without a site-to-site device. Which VPN Gateway connection type fits?", "o": ["Site-to-site", "Point-to-site", "VNet-to-VNet", "ExpressRoute Global Reach"], "a": [1], "e": "Point-to-site VPN connects individual client devices to a VNet. Site-to-site needs an on-premises VPN device."},
{"id": "a51", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about Azure VPN Gateway is TRUE?", "o": ["A VNet can have several VPN gateways, one for each connection", "A VNet has only one VPN gateway, which can serve many connections", "VPN Gateway traffic travels over a dedicated private circuit, not the internet", "VPN gateways can't be used to connect two Azure VNets to each other"], "a": [1], "e": "Each VNet can have one VPN gateway, which can host multiple connections (site-to-site, point-to-site, VNet-to-VNet). Traffic is encrypted over the internet, not a private circuit."},
{"id": "a52", "d": 2, "q": "A VPN device on-premises only supports static routing with one tunnel. Which VPN gateway type is required?", "o": ["Route-based", "Policy-based", "Zone-redundant", "Active-active"], "a": [1], "e": "Policy-based VPNs use static IP prefix lists (policies) and are limited in tunnels and features. Route-based VPNs use routing tables and support most scenarios, including point-to-site and active-active."},
{"id": "a53", "d": 2, "q": "A manufacturer needs a private connection to Azure with predictable latency and up to tens of Gbps, without traffic crossing the public internet. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Site-to-site VPN", "ExpressRoute", "Point-to-site VPN", "VNet peering"], "a": [1], "e": "ExpressRoute provides private connectivity through a connectivity provider that doesn't traverse the public internet, with higher bandwidth and more predictable latency than VPN."},
{"id": "a54", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about ExpressRoute is TRUE?", "o": ["ExpressRoute traffic is encrypted end to end by default with IPsec on the circuit", "ExpressRoute doesn't cross the public internet, but it isn't encrypted by default", "ExpressRoute requires a point-to-site client on each user's PC to use the circuit", "ExpressRoute only connects to Azure VNets and can never reach Microsoft 365 services"], "a": [1], "e": "ExpressRoute is private but not encrypted by default; you can add IPsec or MACsec. Microsoft peering can reach Microsoft 365 and other Microsoft public services."},
{"id": "a55", "d": 2, "q": "A company has offices in London and Tokyo, each with an ExpressRoute circuit. They want the offices to exchange data across the Microsoft network. Which feature fits?", "o": ["ExpressRoute Global Reach", "Global VNet peering", "Point-to-site VPN", "Azure Front Door"], "a": [0], "e": "ExpressRoute Global Reach connects on-premises sites through their ExpressRoute circuits over Microsoft's backbone. Global VNet peering connects VNets, not on-premises sites."},
{"id": "a56", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO are ExpressRoute connectivity models? (Choose two.)", "o": ["CloudExchange colocation", "Any-to-any (IPVPN) networks", "Point-to-site client connections", "VNet-to-VNet over the internet"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "ExpressRoute supports CloudExchange colocation, point-to-point Ethernet, any-to-any (IPVPN), and ExpressRoute Direct. Point-to-site and VNet-to-VNet are VPN Gateway features."},
{"id": "a57", "d": 2, "q": "A company wants VMs in a VNet to reach a specific Azure SQL Database over a private IP address from the VNet, so the database's public access can be disabled. Which feature fits BEST?", "o": ["Private endpoint", "Service endpoint", "Public endpoint with firewall rules", "VNet peering to the SQL service"], "a": [0], "e": "A private endpoint is a NIC with a private IP from your VNet that maps to a specific PaaS resource (via Private Link). You can then disable public network access. Service endpoints keep the service's public IP but restrict traffic to selected subnets."},
{"id": "a58", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement correctly compares private endpoints and service endpoints?", "o": ["Both give the PaaS resource a private IP address inside your VNet's address space", "A private endpoint gives it a private IP in your VNet; a service endpoint doesn't", "Service endpoints work from on-premises over ExpressRoute; private endpoints don't", "Private endpoints cover every instance of a service type; service endpoints cover one"], "a": [1], "e": "Private endpoints bring a specific resource into your VNet with a private IP and work from peered and on-premises networks. Service endpoints extend your VNet identity to the service's public endpoint for a whole service type in that region."},
{"id": "a59", "d": 2, "q": "A storage account has 'Public network access: Enabled from all networks.' What does this mean?", "o": ["Anyone on the internet can read the data in the account without any credentials", "The endpoint is reachable from the internet, but requests still need authorization", "Only Azure services in the same region can reach the account's storage endpoint", "Data in the account is replicated to a public secondary region readable by anyone"], "a": [1], "e": "A public endpoint is network-reachable from the internet, but access still requires authorization (Entra ID, keys, or SAS) unless anonymous blob access is explicitly allowed on a container."},
{"id": "a60", "d": 2, "q": "An on-premises server connected over ExpressRoute private peering must reach an Azure Storage account without using public endpoints. What should the architect use?", "o": ["A service endpoint for Microsoft.Storage on the VNet's subnet", "A private endpoint for the account in a VNet the circuit reaches", "Microsoft peering on the circuit with no additional configuration", "Global VNet peering between the VNet and the storage account"], "a": [1], "e": "Private endpoints can be reached from on-premises over ExpressRoute private peering or VPN. Service endpoints only secure traffic that originates in the subnet, not from on-premises. Peering connects VNets, not PaaS resources."},
{"id": "a61", "d": 2, "q": "A company needs to store millions of images and video files and serve them over HTTP. Which Azure Storage service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Blob Storage", "Azure Queue Storage", "Azure Table Storage", "Azure Files"], "a": [0], "e": "Blob Storage stores massive amounts of unstructured data and serves it over HTTP/HTTPS. Queues are for messages; Tables for NoSQL key/attribute data; Files for SMB/NFS shares."},
{"id": "a62", "d": 2, "q": "A legacy app writes to a mapped drive letter using SMB. The company wants to move the share to Azure with no app changes. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Blob Storage", "Azure Files", "Azure Queue Storage", "Azure Disk Storage"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure Files provides fully managed file shares accessible over SMB (and NFS), so they can be mounted like a network drive."},
{"id": "a63", "d": 2, "q": "An order system needs to decouple a web front end from a back-end processor by passing small messages that are processed asynchronously. Which storage service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Queue Storage", "Azure Table Storage", "Azure Blob Storage", "Azure Files"], "a": [0], "e": "Queue Storage holds large numbers of messages (up to 64 KB each) for asynchronous processing between components."},
{"id": "a64", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about Azure Table Storage is TRUE?", "o": ["It is a relational database that supports joins and foreign keys", "It stores structured NoSQL data with a schemaless key/attribute design", "It provides block-level storage that is attached to VMs as data disks", "It requires an SMB connection from a mapped drive to read and write"], "a": [1], "e": "Table Storage is a NoSQL key/attribute store with a schemaless design. Relational needs Azure SQL; block storage for VMs is Azure Disks."},
{"id": "a65", "d": 2, "q": "Which Azure Storage option is block-level storage designed to be attached to Azure VMs?", "o": ["Azure Files", "Azure Disk Storage", "Azure Blob Storage hot tier", "Azure Table Storage"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure managed disks are block-level storage volumes managed by Azure and attached to VMs."},
{"id": "a66", "d": 2, "q": "Compliance logs must be retained for seven years and are almost never read. If one is needed, a delay of several hours is acceptable. Which blob tier is MOST cost-effective?", "o": ["Hot", "Cool", "Cold", "Archive"], "a": [3], "e": "Archive is the cheapest storage tier for rarely accessed data and tolerates rehydration latency of up to hours. It has the highest access cost and a 180-day minimum."},
{"id": "a67", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about the Archive access tier is TRUE?", "o": ["Archived blobs can be read immediately, just like blobs in the Hot or Cool tier", "Archived blobs are offline and must be rehydrated to an online tier to be read", "Archive can be set as a storage account's default access tier for new blobs", "Archive has no minimum retention period, so there's no early deletion charge"], "a": [1], "e": "Archive blobs are offline; you must rehydrate them to Hot, Cool, or Cold before reading, which can take hours. Archive can't be the account default, and it has a 180-day minimum with early deletion charges."},
{"id": "a68", "d": 2, "q": "What are the minimum retention periods before early deletion charges for the Cool, Cold, and Archive tiers?", "o": ["7, 30, and 90 days", "30, 90, and 180 days", "30, 60, and 365 days", "No minimums for any tier"], "a": [1], "e": "Cool has a 30-day minimum, Cold a 90-day minimum, and Archive a 180-day minimum. Deleting or moving data out earlier incurs an early deletion charge."},
{"id": "a69", "d": 2, "q": "A media company has files that are read heavily for 30 days, then rarely for a year, then almost never. They want tiering to happen automatically. Which feature fits BEST?", "o": ["Lifecycle management policies", "Azure File Sync cloud tiering", "AzCopy sync jobs", "Storage account failover"], "a": [0], "e": "Blob lifecycle management rules move blobs between tiers (or delete them) automatically based on age or last access. File Sync cloud tiering is for file servers; AzCopy is a copy tool."},
{"id": "a70", "d": 2, "q": "Which storage tier has the LOWEST storage cost but the HIGHEST data access cost?", "o": ["Hot", "Cool", "Cold", "Archive"], "a": [3], "e": "As you go from Hot to Archive, storage cost falls and access cost rises. Archive is cheapest to store and most expensive to read."},
{"id": "a71", "d": 2, "q": "How does locally redundant storage (LRS) protect data?", "o": ["Three copies spread across three availability zones in the region", "Three copies within a single datacenter in the primary region", "Six copies split evenly across the primary and a paired region", "One copy, plus nightly backups to a second region for recovery"], "a": [1], "e": "LRS keeps three synchronous copies within one datacenter. It protects against drive and rack failure but not datacenter or region loss."},
{"id": "a72", "d": 2, "q": "A company needs storage that survives the loss of a single datacenter in the primary region, with synchronous replication, but doesn't need a secondary region. Which option fits BEST?", "o": ["LRS", "ZRS", "GRS", "RA-GRS"], "a": [1], "e": "ZRS synchronously replicates across three availability zones in the primary region, surviving a zone (datacenter) failure. GRS/RA-GRS add an asynchronous secondary region."},
{"id": "a73", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about geo-redundant storage (GRS) is TRUE?", "o": ["Data is replicated synchronously to the secondary region on every write", "Data uses LRS in the primary and is copied asynchronously to the secondary", "Data in the secondary region can always be read without a failover", "The secondary region is any region the customer picks at creation time"], "a": [1], "e": "GRS uses LRS in the primary region and asynchronously copies data to the paired secondary region (also LRS there). The secondary isn't readable unless you use RA-GRS or fail over. The secondary is the paired region."},
{"id": "a74", "d": 2, "q": "An app needs to read data from the secondary region even when the primary is healthy, and must survive both a zone outage and a regional outage. Which redundancy option fits BEST?", "o": ["GZRS", "RA-GZRS", "RA-GRS", "ZRS"], "a": [1], "e": "RA-GZRS replicates across zones in the primary region and asynchronously to a secondary region, with read access to the secondary at any time. GZRS lacks read access to the secondary; RA-GRS uses LRS in the primary (no zone resiliency)."},
{"id": "a75", "d": 2, "q": "Which redundancy option provides the HIGHEST durability design target?", "o": ["LRS (at least 11 nines)", "ZRS (at least 12 nines)", "GRS/GZRS (at least 16 nines)", "All options have identical durability"], "a": [2], "e": "Microsoft designs LRS for at least 11 nines, ZRS for at least 12 nines, and GRS/GZRS for at least 16 nines of durability over a year."},
{"id": "a76", "d": 2, "q": "A storage account uses GRS. The primary region has an outage. What is TRUE about data written just before the outage?", "o": ["It's guaranteed to be in the secondary, because geo-replication is synchronous", "Some recent writes may be missing, because geo-replication is asynchronous", "It's automatically restored from Azure Backup into the secondary region", "It's replicated to every region in the geography, so nothing can be lost"], "a": [1], "e": "Geo-replication is asynchronous, so there's a recovery point objective; the most recent writes may be lost if you fail over."},
{"id": "a77", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO statements about storage account names are TRUE? (Choose two.)", "o": ["They must be globally unique across Azure", "They can contain only lowercase letters and numbers, 3 to 24 characters", "They can include hyphens and uppercase letters", "They only need to be unique within the resource group"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Storage account names must be globally unique (they form a DNS endpoint) and use 3-24 lowercase letters and numbers only."},
{"id": "a78", "d": 2, "q": "Which storage account type is recommended for most scenarios and supports Blob, Files, Queues, and Tables with all redundancy options?", "o": ["Standard general-purpose v2", "Premium block blobs", "Premium file shares", "Premium page blobs"], "a": [0], "e": "Standard general-purpose v2 supports blobs (including Data Lake Storage), Files, Queues, and Tables, with LRS, ZRS, GRS, RA-GRS, GZRS, and RA-GZRS. Premium types are specialized and support fewer redundancy options."},
{"id": "a79", "d": 2, "q": "A workload needs low-latency, high-transaction blob storage and also needs zone redundancy. Which storage account type and redundancy combination is valid?", "o": ["Premium block blobs with GRS", "Premium block blobs with ZRS", "Premium page blobs with GZRS", "Premium file shares with RA-GRS"], "a": [1], "e": "Premium block blob accounts support LRS and ZRS. Premium accounts don't support geo-redundant options."},
{"id": "a80", "d": 2, "q": "An admin needs to script a one-time copy of 5 TB of blobs from one storage account to another using the command line. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["AzCopy", "Azure File Sync", "Azure Data Box", "Azure Migrate"], "a": [0], "e": "AzCopy is a command-line tool for copying blobs and files to, from, and between storage accounts. File Sync is for caching file shares on Windows Server; Data Box is offline transfer; Migrate is for workload migration."},
{"id": "a81", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about the AzCopy sync command is TRUE?", "o": ["It synchronizes changes in both directions between source and destination", "It synchronizes in one direction, from source to destination", "It continuously replicates changes in real time as a service", "It only works with Azure Files, not Blob Storage"], "a": [1], "e": "AzCopy sync is one-direction: it makes the destination match the source. It runs when invoked, not as a background service, and works with blobs and files."},
{"id": "a82", "d": 2, "q": "A non-technical team member needs a GUI on macOS to browse containers, upload files, and manage blobs across several storage accounts. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Storage Explorer", "AzCopy", "Azure File Sync", "Azure Cloud Shell"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure Storage Explorer is a cross-platform (Windows, macOS, Linux) GUI for managing storage. It uses AzCopy under the hood for transfers."},
{"id": "a83", "d": 2, "q": "A company has file servers in 12 branch offices and wants one central Azure file share, with a fast local cache at each branch that keeps only frequently used files on local disk. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure File Sync with cloud tiering", "AzCopy scheduled tasks at each branch", "Azure Storage Explorer on each file server", "Azure Data Box Disk"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure File Sync turns Windows Servers into caches of an Azure file share. Cloud tiering keeps hot files local and tiers cold files to Azure. Branches can access data using SMB, NFS, or FTPS on the local server."},
{"id": "a84", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO statements about Azure File Sync are TRUE? (Choose two.)", "o": ["You can have as many caches around the world as you need", "A failed local server can be replaced by installing File Sync on a new server", "It only works with Linux servers", "It synchronizes Azure Blob containers to on-premises disks"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "File Sync supports many server endpoints worldwide and quick recovery by syncing a replacement server. It requires Windows Server and works with Azure file shares, not blob containers."},
{"id": "a85", "d": 2, "q": "A company must move 500 TB from a datacenter with a slow WAN link to Azure within a month. Network transfer would take far too long. Which option fits BEST?", "o": ["AzCopy over the existing WAN", "Azure Data Box", "Azure File Sync", "ExpressRoute Global Reach"], "a": [1], "e": "Azure Data Box is a physical, ruggedized device Microsoft ships to you for offline bulk transfer when network transfer is impractical. Multiple devices can be ordered for large datasets."},
{"id": "a86", "d": 2, "q": "Which is a valid use case for exporting data OUT of Azure with Azure Data Box?", "o": ["Sending a copy of data to an on-premises site for disaster recovery", "Live-migrating running VMs from Azure back to on-premises hosts", "Synchronizing file shares continuously with several branch offices", "Replacing an ExpressRoute circuit for nightly incremental backups"], "a": [0], "e": "Data Box supports export scenarios like DR copies to on-premises, security or compliance requirements, and migrating back on-premises or to another provider. It isn't for continuous sync or live migration."},
{"id": "a87", "d": 2, "q": "What happens to a Data Box device's disks after Microsoft uploads the data to Azure?", "o": ["They are shipped back to the customer for reuse", "They are wiped according to NIST 800-88r1 standards", "They are kept as an offline backup for 90 days", "They are encrypted and stored in the paired region"], "a": [1], "e": "After upload, Microsoft performs a secure erase of the device's disks following NIST 800-88r1 guidelines."},
{"id": "a88", "d": 2, "q": "A company wants one hub to discover on-premises servers, assess Azure readiness and cost, and then migrate servers, databases, and web apps. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure Migrate", "Azure Arc", "Azure Site Recovery only", "Azure Advisor"], "a": [0], "e": "Azure Migrate is a unified hub for discovery, assessment, and migration, integrating tools like Data Migration Assistant, Database Migration Service, Web app migration assistant, and Data Box."},
{"id": "a89", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO tools are integrated into Azure Migrate? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Azure Database Migration Service", "Data Migration Assistant", "Azure Advisor", "Azure Policy"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Azure Migrate integrates Data Migration Assistant (assesses SQL Server), Azure Database Migration Service (moves databases), Web app migration assistant, Azure Data Box, and more. Advisor and Policy are governance and optimization tools."},
{"id": "a90", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement BEST describes Microsoft Entra ID?", "o": ["A domain controller service you deploy and patch in Azure VMs for apps to use", "A cloud identity and access management service used to sign in to apps", "A file sharing service that stores users' home directories and profiles", "A public certificate authority that issues TLS certificates for websites"], "a": [1], "e": "Microsoft Entra ID is Microsoft's cloud-based identity and access management service, used to sign in to Azure, Microsoft 365, and thousands of SaaS and custom apps."},
{"id": "a91", "d": 2, "q": "A company migrates a legacy app to Azure VMs. The app needs domain join, Group Policy, LDAP, and Kerberos, but the company doesn't want to deploy or patch domain controllers in Azure. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Microsoft Entra ID alone", "Microsoft Entra Domain Services", "Microsoft Entra Connect", "Azure AD B2C"], "a": [1], "e": "Microsoft Entra Domain Services provides managed domain services (domain join, Group Policy, LDAP, Kerberos/NTLM) without you managing domain controllers. Entra ID by itself doesn't provide LDAP or Kerberos to VMs."},
{"id": "a92", "d": 2, "q": "How does data flow between Microsoft Entra ID and a Microsoft Entra Domain Services managed domain?", "o": ["Two-way sync; changes made in the managed domain write back to Entra ID", "One-way sync from Entra ID to the managed domain", "No sync; users must be created separately in each", "One-way sync from the managed domain to Entra ID"], "a": [1], "e": "Entra Domain Services syncs one way from Entra ID. Users, groups, and credentials created in Entra ID (including those synced from on-premises) appear in the managed domain; changes in the managed domain don't sync back."},
{"id": "a93", "d": 2, "q": "A company wants users' on-premises AD identities available in Microsoft Entra ID so they can use the same credentials for Microsoft 365. Which tool fits BEST?", "o": ["Microsoft Entra Connect (or Cloud Sync)", "Microsoft Entra Domain Services", "Azure Migrate", "Azure Arc-enabled servers"], "a": [0], "e": "Microsoft Entra Connect and Entra Cloud Sync synchronize identities from on-premises AD to Entra ID. Domain Services goes the other way for VMs that need domain features."},
{"id": "a94", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about single sign-on (SSO) is MOST accurate?", "o": ["SSO means users no longer have any password, since apps trust the device instead", "SSO lets users sign in once for many apps, so that one identity matters more", "SSO increases the number of credentials users must remember across their apps", "SSO only works with on-premises apps that are joined to an Active Directory domain"], "a": [1], "e": "SSO lets one identity unlock many apps. It reduces credential sprawl but makes that identity more valuable, which is why SSO is usually paired with MFA."},
{"id": "a95", "d": 2, "q": "Which combination is a valid example of multifactor authentication?", "o": ["A password and a security question", "A password and an Authenticator app code", "Two different passwords entered in sequence", "A PIN and a password typed at the same prompt"], "a": [1], "e": "MFA requires factors from different categories: something you know, something you have, something you are. A password plus an app code (something you have) qualifies. A password plus security question are both 'know.'"},
{"id": "a96", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO are passwordless authentication methods supported by Microsoft Entra ID? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Windows Hello for Business", "FIDO2 security keys (passkeys)", "SMS one-time codes combined with a password", "Security questions"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Passwordless options include Windows Hello for Business, the Microsoft Authenticator app, and FIDO2 security keys/passkeys. SMS with a password and security questions aren't passwordless."},
{"id": "a97", "d": 2, "q": "A partner company's employees need to access a SharePoint site and an internal app using their own corporate credentials, without the host company creating new passwords for them. Which feature fits BEST?", "o": ["Microsoft Entra B2B collaboration", "Microsoft Entra Domain Services", "Microsoft Entra Connect", "Azure RBAC custom roles"], "a": [0], "e": "B2B collaboration invites external users as guests who sign in with their own identities. The host manages their access without managing their credentials."},
{"id": "a98", "d": 2, "q": "A retailer is building a customer-facing app and wants shoppers to sign up with email or social accounts like Google, with a branded sign-in experience. Which option fits BEST?", "o": ["Microsoft Entra External ID for customers", "Microsoft Entra B2B collaboration", "Microsoft Entra Domain Services", "Microsoft Entra Connect"], "a": [0], "e": "Customer (consumer) identity scenarios use Microsoft Entra External ID (the successor to Azure AD B2C) for sign-up and sign-in with local or social accounts. B2B collaboration is for business partners."},
{"id": "a99", "d": 2, "q": "Two organizations both use Microsoft Entra ID and want users to collaborate in Teams shared channels without either side creating guest accounts. Which feature fits?", "o": ["B2B direct connect", "B2B collaboration with guest invites", "Entra Domain Services trust", "Azure Lighthouse"], "a": [0], "e": "B2B direct connect establishes a mutual two-way trust between Entra organizations, used for Teams Connect shared channels, without adding guests to each directory."},
{"id": "a100", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement BEST describes Microsoft Entra Conditional Access?", "o": ["A cloud firewall that blocks network traffic to Azure VMs based on source location and port", "A policy engine that uses signals like user, location, device, and risk to make access decisions", "A role assignment model that grants permissions on Azure resources at a scope such as a group", "A data protection tool that encrypts files and emails based on their sensitivity labels"], "a": [1], "e": "Conditional Access evaluates signals (who, where, what device, what app, risk) and makes access decisions: allow, block, or require MFA, a compliant device, and so on."},
{"id": "a101", "d": 2, "q": "A company wants to require MFA only when users sign in from outside the corporate network, and block sign-ins from a country where it has no business. Which feature fits BEST?", "o": ["Azure RBAC", "Microsoft Entra Conditional Access", "Network security groups", "Azure Policy"], "a": [1], "e": "Conditional Access policies can use location (named locations, countries) to require MFA or block access. RBAC grants resource permissions, NSGs filter network traffic, and Azure Policy governs resource configuration."},
{"id": "a102", "d": 2, "q": "Which licensing is required to use Microsoft Entra Conditional Access?", "o": ["Microsoft Entra ID Free", "Microsoft Entra ID P1 or P2", "Any paid Azure subscription", "Microsoft Defender for Cloud Standard"], "a": [1], "e": "Conditional Access requires Microsoft Entra ID P1 (or P2, which adds risk-based policies). It's also included in suites like Microsoft 365 E3/E5 and Business Premium."},
{"id": "a103", "d": 2, "q": "An Azure role assignment is made up of which three elements?", "o": ["User, password, and region", "Security principal, role definition, and scope", "Subscription, resource group, and resource tag", "Policy definition, initiative, and policy effect"], "a": [1], "e": "A role assignment ties a security principal (user, group, service principal, managed identity) to a role definition (set of permissions) at a scope (management group, subscription, resource group, or resource)."},
{"id": "a104", "d": 2, "q": "A help desk group needs to restart VMs and view their status in one resource group, but must not delete them or change networking. What is the BEST approach?", "o": ["Assign Owner on the subscription so the group is never blocked", "Assign Contributor on the resource group that holds the VMs", "Assign a role with only the needed actions, scoped to the group", "Add the help desk group to the Global Administrator directory role"], "a": [2], "e": "Least privilege means granting only the needed actions at the narrowest scope. Owner and Contributor allow deletes; Global Administrator is an Entra directory role, not an Azure resource role."},
{"id": "a105", "d": 2, "q": "What is the key difference between the Owner and Contributor built-in roles?", "o": ["Contributor can delete resources; Owner can't", "Owner can also manage access (assign roles); Contributor can't", "Owner is limited to one resource group; Contributor isn't", "Contributor can read billing; Owner can't"], "a": [1], "e": "Both can create, change, and delete resources. Owner can also assign roles to others; Contributor can't grant access."},
{"id": "a106", "d": 2, "q": "Which built-in role lets someone manage user access to Azure resources WITHOUT being able to manage the resources themselves?", "o": ["Reader", "Contributor", "User Access Administrator", "Security Reader"], "a": [2], "e": "User Access Administrator can manage role assignments but doesn't grant resource management rights. Reader views; Contributor manages resources but not access."},
{"id": "a107", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about how Azure RBAC is enforced is TRUE?", "o": ["It's enforced by the guest OS on each VM when users sign in", "It's enforced on actions that go through Azure Resource Manager", "It only applies to resources that were created through the portal", "It blocks or allows network traffic between peered virtual networks"], "a": [1], "e": "RBAC is enforced on any action initiated against Azure resources through Azure Resource Manager (portal, CLI, PowerShell, SDKs, REST). It doesn't control guest OS logins or network flows."},
{"id": "a108", "d": 2, "q": "Which TWO are core principles of Zero Trust? (Choose two.)", "o": ["Verify explicitly", "Assume breach", "Trust the internal network", "Grant broad access to reduce friction"], "a": [0, 1], "e": "Zero Trust has three guiding principles: verify explicitly, use least privilege access, and assume breach. Trusting the internal network is the old perimeter model it replaces."},
{"id": "a109", "d": 2, "q": "A company's network design trusts any device on the corporate LAN by default. After moving to Zero Trust, what changes MOST?", "o": ["Devices on the LAN are still trusted, but only after a firewall rule allows each one", "Each request is verified using identity, device, and other signals, wherever it comes from", "The corporate LAN becomes the only place users can work from, so remote access is dropped", "A VPN becomes mandatory for every cloud app so all traffic passes through the LAN first"], "a": [1], "e": "Zero Trust drops implicit trust based on network location. Each request is verified explicitly using identity, device health, location, and other signals."},
{"id": "a110", "d": 2, "q": "In the defense-in-depth model, which layer is at the CENTER, the thing all other layers protect?", "o": ["Physical security", "Identity and access", "Network", "Data"], "a": [3], "e": "The layers from outside in are physical security, identity and access, perimeter, network, compute, application, and data. Data is at the center."},
{"id": "a111", "d": 2, "q": "Which control belongs to the PERIMETER layer of defense in depth?", "o": ["Multifactor authentication for all admins", "DDoS protection and perimeter firewalls", "Encrypting data at rest with managed keys", "Patching the virtual machines' guest OS"], "a": [1], "e": "The perimeter layer uses DDoS protection to filter large-scale attacks and perimeter firewalls to identify and alert on malicious traffic. MFA is identity, encryption is data, VM patching is compute."},
{"id": "a112", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement BEST describes the purpose of the defense-in-depth model?", "o": ["To rely on one very strong control at the perimeter so other controls aren't needed", "To layer controls so that if one layer is breached, others still slow or stop the attack", "To move all security responsibility to the cloud provider once workloads are migrated", "To replace identity-based controls with network controls that are easier to audit"], "a": [1], "e": "Defense in depth layers multiple controls so a single failure doesn't expose the data. It slows attackers and gives defenders time to detect and respond."},
{"id": "a113", "d": 2, "q": "A company wants one service that assesses the security posture of its Azure, AWS, and on-premises servers, provides a secure score, and offers threat protection for workloads. Which service fits BEST?", "o": ["Microsoft Defender for Cloud", "Azure Advisor", "Azure Policy", "Microsoft Purview"], "a": [0], "e": "Defender for Cloud provides cloud security posture management (secure score, recommendations) and workload protection across Azure, other clouds (AWS, GCP), and on-premises (via Azure Arc)."},
{"id": "a114", "d": 2, "q": "Which statement about Microsoft Defender for Cloud is TRUE?", "o": ["It only protects Azure resources, not other clouds or on-premises servers", "Foundational posture management is free; workload protection plans are paid", "It replaces Microsoft Entra ID as the identity provider for Azure resources", "It is only available to customers running in the Azure Government cloud"], "a": [1], "e": "Defender for Cloud includes free foundational CSPM (secure score, recommendations), with paid Defender plans for servers, storage, databases, containers, and more. It supports multicloud and hybrid."}
);
